      <h1>Colophon</h1>
      <p class="lede">A nocturne archive. Commentary, not catalogue.</p>
    </header>

    <article class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        <strong>oriz·lore</strong> is what's left after I close a book — the structured
        commentary I keep, so I don't have to remember it from scratch the next time.
        Read it by lamplight.
      </p>

      <h2>Four sections, one volume</h2>
      <p>
        Each entry breaks into four MDX artifacts: <em>overview</em>, <em>content map</em>,
        <em>analysis</em>, and <em>narration</em>. Sections are written as the book deserves
        — not on a schedule. A volume can sit at three-of-four for years until rereading
        unlocks the fourth.
      </p>

      <h2>Set for reading in the dark</h2>
      <p>
        The page is a darkened blue-black slate lit by a single gilt reading lamp; body
        text is aged vellum in <em>Spectral</em>, headings in <em>Fraunces</em>. Hand notes
        hang in the margin in foxed-paper rust — a record of where I was when I read it,
        what I disagreed with, what I came back to. A verdigris ribbon down the left edge of
        every book page silently remembers where you stopped reading.
      </p>

      <h2>Free to read, yours to keep</h2>
      <p>
        Every summary is public — no sign-in to read, no paywall, nothing gated. Sign in only
        if you want a personal shelf: mark volumes to return to, kept to your account across
        every oriz.in site. The reading itself never asks who you are.
      </p>

      <h2>Not a substitute</h2>
      <p>
        These entries are commentary; the book itself is the territory. If a summary
        intrigues you, please buy the original — the author did the hard work. Quoted
        excerpts fall under fair use / fair dealing. For specific concerns from authors or
        rights-holders, the <a href="/legal/grievance/">grievance officer</a> page is the
        formal channel.
      </p>

      <h2>Part of the oriz family</h2>
      <p>
        oriz·lore is one of several small sites under <a href="https://oriz.in">oriz.in</a>,
        a free family of static sites by Chirag Singhal.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href="mailto:whyiswhen@gmail.com">whyiswhen@gmail.com</a> or use the
        <a href="/contact/">contact form</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
